AnalystLab Africa — Generative AI Internship
Week 2 — Problem Definition Report
Project: AI Customer Support Assistant for ABC Communications Ltd — Addis Ababa, Ethiopia
Business Problem
ABC Communications Ltd, a telecom provider in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, faces a support team overloaded with repetitive customer questions about billing, data plans, and technical troubleshooting. This drives long wait times, higher support costs, and weaker customer retention in a newly competitive telecom market. This project builds a functional prototype of an AI-powered support assistant that accepts a customer's question, sends it to a Large Language Model (LLM), and returns a grounded, accurate response — demonstrating a practical, working application of Generative AI rather than just a design on paper.
Target Users
ABC Communications' existing customers across Addis Ababa and surrounding areas — a mix of urban, tech-savvy customers who prefer chat-based support, and less tech-savvy customers who need clear, simple answers without technical jargon.
Proposed AI Solution
A command-line / notebook-based AI assistant (“Tena”) built in Python using Google's Gemini API (free tier, no credit card required). The application:
•Accepts a customer's question as text input
•Validates the input (rejects empty, too-short, or excessively long input with a clear message)
•Grounds every response in a local knowledge base of ABC's real plans, pricing, and troubleshooting steps (a lightweight RAG-style approach), instead of letting the LLM rely on its own memory
•Escalates billing disputes, refunds, and complaints to a human agent rather than attempting to resolve them itself
•Handles API errors (e.g. temporary service outages) gracefully via try/except, without crashing
Expected Benefits
•Faster answers to common questions (plans, troubleshooting, payment methods) without waiting for a human agent
•Reduced load on ABC's human support team, freeing them for complex or sensitive cases
•Consistent, accurate answers grounded in real company data rather than guesses
•A foundation that can later be connected to ABC's real account systems for personalized answers
Potential Challenges
•Free-tier API limits (requests per minute/day) could throttle the assistant under real customer volume
•Occasional third-party API outages (observed directly during testing as a 503 “high demand” error) require graceful fallback handling
•The current knowledge base is static and hardcoded; a production version would need to stay synced with ABC's actual live pricing and policies
•English-only prompting risks underserving Amharic-speaking customers unless multilingual support is added
